```

</MermaidZoom>

A notification is a delivery hint, not confirmation that an order succeeded. The application must obtain protected business information through its authorized API. Do not treat a successful SNS publish result as device receipt or user acknowledgement.

## 2. Keep the identifiers separate

| Value | Meaning | Used for |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| Provider token | Current FCM/APNs token for the app installation/provider configuration. | Registering/updating the SNS endpoint. |
| Platform Application ARN | SNS object configured for a provider/application. | Creating a platform endpoint. |
| Endpoint ARN / PushJS `deviceArn` | SNS destination representing the registered app/device endpoint. | Direct publishing or subscribing that endpoint to a topic. |
| Topic ARN | SNS topic representing an approved broadcast/segment destination. | Subscribing endpoints and publishing to the topic. |
| Subscription ARN | Association between an endpoint and a topic. | Unsubscribing that specific association. |

**A Platform Application ARN is not a Topic ARN.** Confirm the resource type before using configuration values in `subscribe` or topic `publish` calls. Do not infer the type from an environment variable's name. [SNS mobile API actions](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sns/latest/dg/mobile-push-api.html).

Store `deviceArn` and subscription identifiers separately. The Node.js integration documents user lookup by Enterprise email; that lookup must remain protected backend data, not notification content or a substitute for request authorization.

Tokens can change and endpoints can become disabled. Registration needs an owner-defined update/reconciliation lifecycle; repeatedly creating an endpoint from a permanently cached token is insufficient. [SNS endpoint lifecycle](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sns/latest/dg/mobile-platform-endpoint.html).

## 3. Obtain configuration before writing code

Ask the backend/platform owner for these values through approved channels:

| Required information | What to confirm |
| :--- | :--- |
| AWS environment and Region | Correct account/environment and configured SNS Region; do not copy a sample Region without confirmation. |
| Platform applications | Separate approved FCM/APNs Platform Application ARNs, matching the actual mobile configurations. |
| Optional topics | Actual Topic ARNs, authorized audiences, and subscription policy. |
| Backend authentication | Approved runtime AWS credential/role binding and permitted SNS operations. No embedded access keys. |
| Registration contract | Actual authenticated mobile-to-backend API, token/provider type, ownership validation, and update/logout handling. |
| Storage | Existing user/device/endpoint/subscription persistence contracts. |
| Node.js package access | Approved registry, PushJS version, and protected CI package-authentication mechanism. |

Confirm the mobile SDK/configuration method separately for each app. The backend material does not define the mobile registration endpoint schema, token-refresh API, logout cleanup contract, or deep-link route format. Obtain those contracts from the application owner before implementing them. Do not invent a `/register-device` API or route schema from this overview.

**Expected result:** you can distinguish the configured platform applications from topics and identify the existing registration/persistence boundaries. If a binding is missing, resolve it before executing registration or sending a message.

## 4. Choose the mobile delivery method

| Method | Mobile registration | Backend destination | Setup page |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| FCM — Android | FCM registration token. | Approved FCM sender or SNS FCM platform endpoint. | `fcm-mobile-setup.mdx`, Android tab. |
| FCM — iOS | FCM registration token, with APNs configured beneath Firebase. | Approved FCM sender or SNS FCM platform endpoint; not the direct APNs endpoint. | `fcm-mobile-setup.mdx`, iOS tab. |
| Direct APNs — iOS | APNs device token matching the app/provider environment. | Approved APNs sender or SNS APNs platform endpoint. | `apns-ios-setup.mdx`. |
| React Native | The selected Android/iOS native integration and its provider token. | The matching approved backend path above. | `fcm-mobile-setup.mdx`, React Native tab; native APNs integration where selected. |

Choose one coherent registration/handling path per application configuration. Supporting multiple methods across Enterprise apps does not mean every app must initialize all methods.

<Tabs>
<TabItem label="Android">

Follow FCM mobile setup: Firebase app registration, approved dependency/configuration, permission/channel handling, token lifecycle, foreground/background receipt, and notification taps.

The page will provide `FcmService.kt` and `NotificationService.kt` templates with their manifest and application integration points. Backend registration and authenticated routing remain explicit host-provided contracts.

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="iOS">

Choose **FCM through APNs** or **direct APNs** before configuring the app. Both require the appropriate Apple capabilities/provider setup; their backend tokens differ.

The FCM page will provide `FcmService.swift` and `NotificationService.swift`. The direct APNs page will provide its separate registration/notification templates. Preserve the existing application and notification delegate ownership instead of registering competing delegates.

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="React Native">

Use the FCM page's platform-specific native setup plus `FcmService.ts` and `NotificationService.ts`, the background entry point, and root/listener wiring. Keep existing Redux Toolkit state ownership.

Android and iOS permission, token, background, and cold-start behavior must be verified separately. Tokens and backend credentials do not belong in Redux state or telemetry. A direct APNs integration is a separate native path, not an assumed option of an unspecified JavaScript package.

</TabItem>
</Tabs>

### Choose the backend implementation

| Backend | Procedure |
| :--- | :--- |
| Java with SNS | `java-backend.mdx`: configured platform applications, endpoint registration/reconciliation, topic subscriptions, platform payloads, and publishing. |
| Node.js with PushJS | `nodejs-pushjs-backend.mdx`: approved package access, device registration, direct/topic publishing, subscription lifecycle, and persistence contracts. |

Use the existing backend stack. Sending credentials and provider publishing operations stay on the trusted backend; the mobile service templates must not embed them.

Notification foundation responsibilities are described in [App Foundations](../enterprise-app-foundations.mdx). Its illustrative adapters are not a versioned internal SDK installation contract.

## 5. Prepare one safe test notification

Use a dedicated non-production app installation and synthetic content. Agree the recipient, provider, and expected app-state behavior before any live send.

This is a **test-planning record**, not a provider payload, backend API schema, or ready-to-run send command:

```text
Application/environment: [Approved non-production app]
Platform/provider: [Android FCM / iOS APNs, matching actual configuration]
Backend implementation: [Java SNS / Node.js PushJS]
Recipient: [Approved test record; do not paste a token into documentation]
Delivery path: [Direct endpoint / authorized topic]
Title: My Chicken App
Body: An update is available. Open the app to view it.
App state: [Foreground / background / opened from notification]
Expected behavior: [Approved platform-specific presentation/handling]
Evidence: [Backend result and observed device behavior, recorded separately]
```

Do not put a real order ID, customer name, address, price, or payment detail into a lock-screen message. If routing requires an identifier, its approved schema and authorization behavior must be defined separately.

Keep payloads small. Firebase documents a **4,096-byte maximum for notification and data message payloads**, with a separate composer limit; do not apply a universal 2 KB notification-message limit. Topic-specific and other provider/envelope constraints still apply; see [Notification Guidelines](./notification-guidelines.mdx) before choosing a size limit. [FCM message types and size limits](https://firebase.google.com/docs/cloud-messaging/customize-messages/set-message-type).

**Attach screenshot — sanitized test notification and separate backend/device results, with tokens and identifiers hidden.**

