---
title: "Notification Guidelines"
description: "Design small, private notification payloads, respect user preferences, and verify provider acceptance separately from device behavior."
sidebar:
  label: Notification Guidelines
  order: 2
---

import { Tabs, TabItem } from '@astrojs/starlight/components';
import MermaidZoom from '../components/mermaid-zoom.astro';

Read [Start Here](./index.mdx) first. Apply these rules before configuring FCM/direct APNs mobile services, implementing the Java or Node.js backend, or sending a non-production test.

**Outcome:** a small, permitted message sent to the authorized audience, with delivery and device behavior recorded separately.

> **Confidential — Enterprise internal use only.** Notification content can appear on a locked screen. Keep customer/order details, credentials, private identifiers, and backend payloads out of message text and diagnostic logs.

## Service responsibilities

Keep the mobile templates focused on two responsibilities. Platform configuration, application startup, background entry points, and host-provided backend/navigation contracts remain separate integration steps.

| Template | Owns | Must not do |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `FcmService` | FCM registration/token refresh, message callback wiring, and listener lifecycle for the selected platform. | Embed sender credentials, invent registration endpoints, or authorize a business operation from push data. |
| `NotificationService` | Permission/presentation coordination, Android channels where applicable, payload validation, and safe handling of user-open events. | Display the same system notification twice, navigate before startup/authentication, or trust an arbitrary supplied URL. |
| Direct APNs registration service | APNs token registration and native callback integration, separate from the FCM path. | Label an APNs token as an FCM token or initialize competing delegates. |

Receiving, displaying, and opening a message are separate events. If the SDK/OS already displays a notification, application code must not post a second local copy for the same event. Confirm the exact SDK/server payload behavior instead of assuming every message reaches the same callback.

## 1. Decide whether to send

| Decision | Required behavior |
| :--- | :--- |
| Relevance | Send only information useful to the intended recipient. Avoid repeated, spammy, or unrelated messages. |
| Audience | Use the authorized user/device association or approved topic membership. A supplied endpoint ARN alone is not authorization to message that recipient. |
| Preferences | Respect notification categories, opt-in/opt-out choices, and frequency preferences through the established backend contract. |
| Timing | Consider the recipient's time zone and agreed scheduling rules. Do not copy a fixed server time as the user's local time. |
| Personalization | Use permitted preference/segment information without exposing sensitive content or expanding data collection. |
| Feedback | Provide the app's established feedback/preferences route and investigate messages users find excessive or irrelevant. |

A marketing opt-in and an operating-system notification permission are different controls. A provider token is neither proof of opt-in nor permission to add a user to every topic.

For Apple applications, notifications must not be required for the app to function, must not expose sensitive/confidential information, and marketing messages require explicit opt-in plus an opt-out mechanism. Do not monetize the system notification capability or use it for unsolicited/spam content. [Apple App Review Guidelines, sections 3.2.2 and 4.5](https://developer.apple.com/app-store/review/guidelines/).

## 2. Keep payloads small and provider-specific

| Constraint | How to apply it |
| :--- | :--- |
| FCM notification/data messages | Firebase's message-type guide states a 4,096-byte maximum for both types. It separately documents the Firebase console's 1,000-character composer limit. |
| FCM topic-specific limits | The error-code reference documents 2,048 bytes for messages sent to topics. Check the actual provider API/path; this is not a universal limit for every notification message. |
| APNs ordinary remote notifications | Maximum JSON payload is 4,096 bytes. The larger VoIP limit is not applicable to ordinary order-update notifications. |
| SNS envelope | SNS publishing format and provider limits are separate validations. Measure the decoded provider payload as well as validating the outer SNS request. |

[FCM message types](https://firebase.google.com/docs/cloud-messaging/customize-messages/set-message-type), [FCM size errors](https://firebase.google.com/docs/cloud-messaging/error-codes), [APNs payload requirements](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/usernotifications/generating-a-remote-notification).

An **SNS topic** fans out to subscribed platform endpoints. It is not an **FCM topic**, so do not infer provider routing or limits merely from the backend method name `sendMessageToTopic`.

Do not split a large business record into multiple notifications: arrival and order are not guaranteed. Send a minimal update hint; retrieve current protected content through the authenticated application API.

### Safe content example

This JSON illustrates message content only. It is **not** a complete SNS envelope, HTTP v1 request, PushJS schema, or deep-link contract:

```json
{
  "title": "My Chicken App",
  "body": "An update is available. Open the app to view it."
}
```

Do not add a customer's name, address, payment details, order payload, or real order ID to this example. Additional data fields require a defined application contract and privacy review.

### Inspect UTF-8 size locally

Save the actual serialized provider JSON—not an SNS JSON string wrapper—to a local test file. From that file's directory:

```bash
PAYLOAD_PATH='REPLACE_WITH_PROVIDER_PAYLOAD_JSON_PATH'
node - "$PAYLOAD_PATH" <<'JS'
const fs = require('node:fs');
const input = fs.readFileSync(process.argv[2], 'utf8');
const value = JSON.parse(input);
if (value === null || typeof value !== 'object' || Array.isArray(value)) {
  throw new Error('Expected a provider JSON object');
}
const serialized = JSON.stringify(value);
console.log(`Compact provider JSON: ${Buffer.byteLength(serialized, 'utf8')} bytes`);
JS
```

This validates JSON syntax and measures compact serialization without printing payload contents or sending it. Compare with the actual API's accounting rules; the sending library must use the same serialization for this measurement to apply. A byte count does not validate provider schema, reserved keys, audience, or permission.

## 3. Configure expiry and delivery expectations

| Behavior | Developer implication |
| :--- | :--- |
| Delivery can be delayed or fail | Keep the app usable without receiving the push; load current state through the normal API. |
| Messages may arrive out of order | Do not use arrival order to determine the latest order status or apply irreversible business transitions. |
| Collapsing can replace pending updates | Choose collapse behavior only when an older update can be safely replaced. |
| Expiry can discard an undelivered message | Set lifetime according to usefulness; do not display obsolete business information as current. |
| Backend receives a publish result | Record acceptance separately from device receipt, display, tap, and business outcome. |

For Android FCM, TTL can range from zero to 28 days; zero discards messages that cannot be delivered immediately. APNs expiration has its own provider header. Do not add an invented `ttl` field to the private PushJS call: first confirm how its actual version exposes provider options. [FCM lifetime](https://firebase.google.com/docs/cloud-messaging/customize-messages/setting-message-lifespan), [FCM ordering/collapse](https://firebase.google.com/docs/cloud-messaging/customize-messages/collapsible-message-types), [APNs delivery](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/usernotifications/sending-notification-requests-to-apns).

No fixed “real-time” delivery deadline is promised here. An offline or restricted device can miss an update even when the backend accepted the send.

## 4. Respect platform permission and app state

<Tabs>
<TabItem label="Android">

For non-exempt notification presentation, Android 13/API 33 and later use `POST_NOTIFICATIONS` runtime permission. The configured target SDK, existing installation state, and user choice affect the flow. Permission denial must not break ordinary order functionality. [Android notification permission](https://developer.android.com/develop/ui/compose/notifications/notification-permission).

Confirm the existing notification channels, current app permission, actual FCM integration, and compatible device/provider services. Device compatibility is not simply a matter of a phone being “low-end.” Use the selected SDK's documented requirements. [FCM Android prerequisites](https://firebase.google.com/docs/cloud-messaging/android/get-started).

Test foreground and background behavior separately. Notification-plus-data and data-only messages do not have identical handling paths. [FCM message behavior](https://firebase.google.com/docs/cloud-messaging/customize-messages/set-message-type).

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="iOS">

Request alert/sound/badge authorization in the application's appropriate user context and inspect current settings; users can change them later. Follow the app's approved authorization flow rather than repeatedly forcing a prompt. [Apple notification authorization](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/usernotifications/asking-permission-to-use-notifications).

For **direct APNs**, register the APNs token with the matching APNs backend configuration. For **FCM on iOS**, send the FCM registration token to the FCM-configured backend; Firebase must also have the correct APNs setup. Do not interchange these tokens.

Verify the APNs environment and actual signing/entitlement configuration for the installed build. A development token and production platform application must not be treated as interchangeable. Background execution or notification display is not guaranteed merely because APNs accepted a message.

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="React Native">

Apply Android and iOS rules independently through the existing approved native notification integration. Do not install a new package or replace callbacks solely to match an example.

Distinguish permission, provider token, backend endpoint association, message receipt, presentation, and user tap. Background handlers must not assume the navigation tree is mounted. A notification tap must wait for the app's established startup/authentication flow and validate the destination using the real route contract.

Bind the services to the actual registration API, notification library, and route schema. Keep unavailable contracts explicit rather than inventing a backend endpoint or route. Do not store tokens or credentials in Redux state or emit them into telemetry.

</TabItem>
</Tabs>

## 5. Maintain registration and recipient ownership

1. Use the current token from the actual mobile provider integration, not a token copied into a fixture or source file.
2. Register/reconcile it through the application's authenticated backend contract and store the resulting endpoint association.
3. Keep the **Endpoint ARN**, **Topic ARN**, and **Subscription ARN** distinct. Topic subscription uses protocol `application` with the endpoint as the subscriber.
4. Handle token changes, disabled/invalid endpoints, account changes, and opt-out through the owner-defined lifecycle. Do not invent token-refresh/logout endpoints from these instructions.
5. Persist and use the correct subscription identifier for unsubscription. Do not substitute a device endpoint ARN for it.

Do not expose an unauthenticated “send to arbitrary ARN” interface. Resolve recipients using authorized backend records, and avoid logging raw tokens, email mappings, ARNs, or provider responses containing sensitive details. [SNS endpoint lifecycle](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sns/latest/dg/mobile-platform-endpoint.html), [SNS API identifiers](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sns/latest/dg/mobile-push-api.html).

## 6. Verify a controlled journey

<MermaidZoom title="Test preferences, backend acceptance and device behavior separately">

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A["Select authorized non-production app and recipient"] --> B["Confirm preferences, permission and provider mapping"]
    B --> C["Validate minimal payload and actual provider limits"]
    C --> D["Send through the approved backend implementation"]
    D --> E["Record backend/provider acceptance or error"]
    E --> F["Observe device receipt and presentation separately"]
    F --> G["Open notification and verify authorized current app state"]
    G --> H["Record result without tokens or sensitive payloads"]
    classDef setup fill:#172554,stroke:#93c5fd,color:#fff;
    classDef send fill:#4c1d95,stroke:#d8b4fe,color:#fff;
    classDef observe fill:#713f12,stroke:#fde047,color:#fff;
    classDef result fill:#064e3b,stroke:#6ee7b7,color:#fff;
    class A,B,C setup;
    class D,E send;
    class F,G observe;
