```

</MermaidZoom>

Use dedicated synthetic test users/devices. Actual sending is a separate authorized test action, not something a developer performs merely by reading this page.

| Scenario | Expected verification |
| :--- | :--- |
| Foreground | Behavior matches the approved payload/handler configuration; no unintended duplicate UI. |
| Background | Platform presentation/data handling matches the configured message type. |
| Open from notification — already running | Handle the user-open event once and validate the current account/destination. |
| Open from notification — cold start | Retain the pending intent until startup/authentication/navigation are ready, then validate it; do not assume mounting alone proves readiness. |
| Permission denied or category opted out | Core app functionality remains usable and documented preferences are respected. |
| Device temporarily offline | Record eventual delivery or expiry; do not infer immediate failure/success from the send response. |
| Invalid/stale token | Error is classified and endpoint lifecycle is reconciled without a retry storm. |
| Registration refresh or repeated startup | Current token is associated with the correct installation/account, listeners are not duplicated, and failed registration is not reported as success. |
| Different accounts on the same device | Recipient ownership is correct; a notification cannot expose the prior account's data. |
| Multiple OS/device configurations | Verify actual supported configurations and native paths, not just one simulator. |

Record only the necessary evidence:

```text
Application/environment: [Approved non-production identity]
Platform/provider and build: [Actual values]
Test scenario and app state: [Foreground/background/open/denied/offline]
Preferences and permission: [Observed state; no personal payload]
Target record: [Access-controlled reference, not a raw token]
Backend result/time: [Accepted / classified failure / unknown]
Device result/time: [Observed receipt/display/open or unverified]
Expected versus actual: [Specific behavior]
Unresolved contract/owner: [If applicable]
```

**Attach screenshot — sanitized device result and backend status, with recipient identifiers and message payloads protected.**

## 7. Investigate the failing layer

| Observation | Inspect / next action |
| :--- | :--- |
| Send fails before publishing | Validate configuration, provider schema/size, runtime credentials, and target resource type. |
| Publish accepted but no visible alert | Check device connectivity, permission/channel settings, app state, payload type, expiry, and actual provider mapping. |
| Endpoint disabled/invalid | Follow the verified token-update and endpoint reconciliation process; do not repeatedly publish unchanged requests. |
| Wrong user receives an update | Stop the affected test/send path and investigate account ownership and subscription mapping with the owner. |
| Duplicate/out-of-order update | Inspect repeat sends, retry behavior, collapse policy, and app handling; fetch authoritative business state. |
| Topic operation rejects an ARN | Verify it is an SNS Topic ARN, not a Platform Application ARN or Endpoint ARN. |
| Timeout leaves the send outcome unknown | Reconcile available evidence before retrying; another send can create duplicate notifications. |

Use error-specific provider guidance rather than swallowing errors and returning success. [FCM error reference](https://firebase.google.com/docs/cloud-messaging/error-codes).

FCM is listed as a no-cost Firebase product; backend compute, AWS SNS, storage, logging, network, and other services can still incur charges. Do not assume the notification architecture is free or requires a particular Firebase plan without checking its actual service use. [Firebase pricing](https://firebase.google.com/pricing).

For contributions, use the appropriate repository's issue process. For help, use the [Teams support page](../support/support.mdx).

**Next file:** `fcm-mobile-setup.mdx` — from-scratch Android, iOS, and React Native setup with full `FcmService` and `NotificationService` templates, native configuration, application wiring, and verification. Direct APNs uses its separate `apns-ios-setup.mdx` procedure.




