Use this path when the iOS backend sends **directly through APNs**, including an SNS **APNs** platform application. It does not use Firebase Messaging or an FCM registration token. For FCM on Apple devices, follow [FCM Mobile Setup](./fcm-mobile-setup.mdx) instead.

**Outcome:** obtain the current APNs token, pass it to the application's authorized registration flow, present permitted notifications, and handle default taps after app readiness.

> **Confidential — Enterprise internal use only.** Provider keys/certificates remain with the authorized backend/platform owner. Never bundle `.p8` private keys, provider JWTs, server credentials, or raw test tokens in source, logs, screenshots, or AI prompts.

## 1. Confirm the path and required bindings

| Item | Confirm with the responsible owner |
| :--- | :--- |
| App identity | Exact bundle ID, Apple team, signing profile, and Push Notifications capability. |
| Provider environment | Development/sandbox or production, matching the installed build's signed entitlement. |
| Backend destination | Actual direct APNs provider or SNS APNs platform application; not an SNS FCM application. |
| Registration contract | Authenticated backend registration/update, user/device ownership, opt-out, and account replacement. |
| Navigation | Existing authorized generic updates destination and startup/authentication readiness. |

An **APNs device token** addresses an app installation. A provider **authentication token/JWT** authorizes the server connection. Neither is an FCM registration token. The APNs provider topic is normally the app bundle ID; it is not an SNS Topic ARN. [Apple APNs registration](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/usernotifications/registering-your-app-with-apns), [provider authentication](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/usernotifications/establishing-a-token-based-connection-to-apns).

No custom registration API or deep-link schema is introduced here. The template callback names below are local integration points that your app must implement.

## 2. Configure Apple signing and the provider

1. Have the authorized Apple account owner confirm the registered App ID has push notifications enabled.
2. In Xcode, select the actual application target and enable **Signing & Capabilities → Push Notifications**. Confirm the entitlement/profile through [Signing & Release Approval](../app-store-and-distribution/signing-and-release-approval.mdx).
3. Confirm the selected build uses the intended provisioning/team/environment. Do not hand-edit an entitlement to work around an incompatible signing profile.
4. The authorized provider owner configures its approved APNs authentication—token-based key/team/key identifier or the established certificate method. Store private material through the approved backend secret mechanism.
5. For SNS, the owner confirms the matching APNs platform application and credentials. An optional SNS broadcast topic is a separate resource.
6. Do not add Firebase SDKs, Firebase configuration files, or an FCM service merely to implement this direct APNs path.

Apple supports provider authentication using the appropriate APNs credentials; an App Store Connect API key is not automatically an APNs key. [Apple provider-key guidance](https://developer.apple.com/help/account/capabilities/communicate-with-apns-using-authentication-tokens/), [SNS prerequisites](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sns/latest/dg/sns-prerequisites-for-mobile-push-notifications.html).

This baseline handles visible alerts/default taps. Do not add **Background Modes → Remote notifications** unless implementing an approved silent-background-update flow; enabling a capability does not guarantee background execution.

