## 3. Add the two service files

```text
<ios-application-target>/
├── AppDelegate.swift                  # Existing lifecycle; merge callbacks
└── Notifications/
    ├── ApnsService.swift
    └── NotificationService.swift
```

Add both files to the actual app target. The examples use Swift concurrency/UIKit notification APIs; check the application's supported deployment/compiler configuration before integration. Do not lower concurrency checking or create a second app entry point to make a snippet fit.

### `ApnsService.swift`

```swift
import UIKit

@MainActor
final class ApnsService {
    static let shared = ApnsService()
    private var enqueueTokenSync: ((String) -> Void)?
    private var reportFailure: (() -> Void)?
    private var registrationEnabled = false
    private init() {}

    func configure(
        enqueueTokenSync: @escaping (String) -> Void,
        reportFailure: @escaping () -> Void
    ) {
        self.enqueueTokenSync = enqueueTokenSync
        self.reportFailure = reportFailure
    }

    // Call on each approved app launch and after relevant account changes.
    // This requests the current token from APNs; it does not use a local token cache.
    func activateRegistration() {
        guard enqueueTokenSync != nil else { return }
        registrationEnabled = true
        UIApplication.shared.registerForRemoteNotifications()
    }

    // Existing AppDelegate forwards the system callback here.
    func didRegister(deviceToken: Data) {
        guard registrationEnabled else { return }
        guard !deviceToken.isEmpty else {
            reportFailure?()
            return
        }
        let token = deviceToken.map { String(format: "%02x", $0) }.joined()
        // Backend binding resolves the current account and matching APNs environment.
        // Fast, non-throwing handoff; actual network/ownership handling belongs to host.
        enqueueTokenSync?(token)
    }

    func didFailRegistration() {
        guard registrationEnabled else { return }
        reportFailure?()
    }

    // Prevents new callback forwarding; it is NOT backend unsubscription or a
    // guarantee that already queued registration work has been cancelled.
    func deactivateRegistration() {
        registrationEnabled = false
    }
}
```

Register with APNs on each eligible launch to obtain the current token; do not persist a token locally as the authority for future registrations. Store the association securely on the backend. The callback may change after restore/reinstall or other lifecycle events. [Token lifecycle and forwarding](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/usernotifications/registering-your-app-with-apns).

The host handoff must implement reliable registration, safe failure reporting, and account ownership. If it enqueues work, use the approved protected worker design; do not introduce a permanent plain-text token cache or claim enqueue acceptance is completed backend registration.

### `NotificationService.swift`

```swift
import UIKit
@preconcurrency import UserNotifications

// Ordinary application coordinator, NOT a UNNotificationServiceExtension.
@MainActor
final class NotificationService: NSObject, UNUserNotificationCenterDelegate {
    static let shared = NotificationService()
    private var pendingOpen = false
    // Configure from approved foreground preference. false prevents banners/sounds.
    var presentInForeground = false
    private override init() { super.init() }

    func installDelegate() {
        UNUserNotificationCenter.current().delegate = self
    }

    func requestPresentationPermission() async throws -> Bool {
        let center = UNUserNotificationCenter.current()
        let settings = await center.notificationSettings()
        if settings.authorizationStatus == .notDetermined {
            return try await center.requestAuthorization(options: [.alert, .sound, .badge])
        }
        return settings.authorizationStatus == .authorized ||
            settings.authorizationStatus == .provisional ||
            settings.authorizationStatus == .ephemeral
    }

    // Registration and presentation permission are distinct. Call through the
    // app's approved opt-in flow; OS registration may succeed without visible alerts.
    func registerForRemoteNotifications() {
        UIApplication.shared.registerForRemoteNotifications()
    }

    nonisolated func userNotificationCenter(
        _ center: UNUserNotificationCenter,
        willPresent notification: UNNotification
    ) async -> UNNotificationPresentationOptions {
        await MainActor.run {
            self.presentInForeground ? [.banner, .list, .sound] : []
        }
    }

    nonisolated func userNotificationCenter(
        _ center: UNUserNotificationCenter,
        didReceive response: UNNotificationResponse
    ) async {
        guard response.actionIdentifier == UNNotificationDefaultActionIdentifier else { return }
        // No URL/identifier from the payload is executed or passed to navigation.
        await MainActor.run { self.pendingOpen = true }
    }

    func consumeOpenIfReady(openAuthorizedUpdates: () -> Bool) {
        if pendingOpen && openAuthorizedUpdates() { pendingOpen = false }
    }

    func clearPendingOpen() { pendingOpen = false }
}
```

This is an app coordinator, **not** a `UNNotificationServiceExtension`. It uses native presentation rather than posting a second local copy. The default action opens generic updates only after the host accepts it; custom actions, arbitrary payload routes, and rich-media extensions are not enabled.

Permission to present, registration with APNs, and business notification preferences are distinct. A `true` permission result does not prove that every presentation setting is enabled or that the backend can send. [Apple authorization/settings](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/usernotifications/asking-permission-to-use-notifications).

## 4. Wire the existing lifecycle

<Tabs>
<TabItem label="Android">

Direct APNs is an Apple-platform path. Do not use an APNs token/key in Android. Follow the Android tab in [FCM Mobile Setup](./fcm-mobile-setup.mdx).

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="iOS">

### A. Configure during startup

Merge into the existing `AppDelegate.application(_:didFinishLaunchingWithOptions:)`, before notifications can be processed:

```swift
ApnsService.shared.configure(
    enqueueTokenSync: { token in
        // REQUIRED: bind to real authenticated, account-safe token registration.
        // Do not log the token or assume a captured account is still current.
    },
    reportFailure: {
        // REQUIRED: fixed safe failure category, without error/token dumps.
    }
)
NotificationService.shared.installDelegate()
// Set presentInForeground from the approved app preference if appropriate.
```

The callback bodies are required integration points. Empty bodies are not a working backend implementation. Keep callbacks fast/non-throwing and handle actual registration completion/failure in the host's existing state flow.

There is one `UNUserNotificationCenter` delegate. Reconcile any existing delegate and preserve its behavior; do not silently replace another notification SDK's delegate. Install the delegate before returning from application launch. [Notification response handling](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/usernotifications/handling-notifications-and-notification-related-actions).

### B. Forward APNs registration callbacks

Merge with existing AppDelegate implementations:

```swift
import UIKit

func application(
    _ application: UIApplication,
    didRegisterForRemoteNotificationsWithDeviceToken deviceToken: Data
) {
    ApnsService.shared.didRegister(deviceToken: deviceToken)
}

func application(
    _ application: UIApplication,
    didFailToRegisterForRemoteNotificationsWithError error: Error
) {
    ApnsService.shared.didFailRegistration()
}
```

Do not convert the token using `Data.description` or assume its length. The helper generates hexadecimal bytes for the host backend contract. If the actual API uses a different representation, adapt its boundary deliberately.

### C. Request presentation permission and activate registration

In the appropriate **main-actor user preference flow**:

```swift
Task { @MainActor in
    do {
        let allowed = try await NotificationService.shared.requestPresentationPermission()
        // Update the real UI from allowed. Denial must not block ordering.
        // After the app's separate approved opt-in/account decision:
        ApnsService.shared.activateRegistration()
    } catch {
        // Report a safe permission-request error; do not mark registration successful.
    }
}
```

Bind activation to the real business opt-in policy rather than blindly activating for every user. After an approved opt-in has already been established, register again on eligible launches/account reconciliation; repeatedly requesting OS authorization will not repeatedly show the system prompt.

### D. Consume an open after readiness

At the actual startup/authentication/navigation-ready boundary:

```swift
NotificationService.shared.consumeOpenIfReady {
    // Invoke the existing authorized generic updates action.
    false // REQUIRED replacement: return true only after the host accepts it.
}
```

Call again when readiness changes. The pending flag is in memory and coalesces opens to a single generic intent; it is not a durable per-notification queue. On logout/account replacement, clear pending state, deactivate forwarding, and apply the backend's actual disassociation/cancellation contract. Already queued/in-flight work requires current-account enforcement; deactivation cannot undo a server mutation.

For SwiftUI, use the existing `@UIApplicationDelegateAdaptor` integration when needed. Preserve the actual app entry point, account-session lifecycle, and navigation owner.

</TabItem>
<TabItem label="React Native">

A React Native app can have a direct APNs native integration, but the Swift coordinator alone does not provide a JavaScript API or integrate with its notification library.

Use the existing approved bridge/library and actual delegate ownership. Do not install Firebase to obtain an APNs token for this path, or paste these services alongside an SDK that already owns the same delegate without reconciliation. A copyable JavaScript bridge needs the app's confirmed native-module and event contract; it is not invented here.

If the selected implementation is React Native Firebase, use its [FCM setup](./fcm-mobile-setup.mdx) and matching token/backend configuration instead.

</TabItem>
</Tabs>

## 5. Match the backend and payload

The provider receives the current APNs token through the actual authenticated registration API. For SNS, endpoint registration uses the correct **APNs Platform Application ARN**; direct publishing targets its **Endpoint ARN**. An SNS topic fan-out uses its separate **Topic ARN**. [SNS mobile identifiers](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sns/latest/dg/mobile-push-api.html).

Example **ordinary APNs payload** with synthetic content:

```json
{
  "aps": {
    "alert": {
      "title": "My Chicken App",
      "body": "An update is available. Open the app to view it."
    },
    "sound": "default"
  }
}
```

This JSON is the APNs payload, not a complete HTTP request or SNS envelope. Sender authentication, device token/path, `apns-topic`, push type, priority, expiration, and environment belong in the approved provider integration. Do not place an `aps` dictionary inside itself or embed credentials in the payload. Ordinary APNs payloads have a 4,096-byte limit. [Apple payload and request guidance](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/usernotifications/generating-a-remote-notification), [sending requests](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/usernotifications/sending-notification-requests-to-apns).

No live send command is provided: the authorized sender must use the actual backend/provider contract. Do not duplicate a send after a timeout without reconciling the unknown outcome.

## 6. Verify registration, presentation, and opening

<MermaidZoom title="Direct APNs registration does not pass through Firebase">

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant APP as iOS App / AppDelegate
    participant APNS as APNs
    participant SERVICE as ApnsService
    participant BACKEND as Authorized Backend / SNS
    participant UI as NotificationService / App Navigation
    APP->>APNS: Register after approved activation
    APNS-->>APP: Current device token or registration failure
    APP->>SERVICE: Forward native callback
    SERVICE->>BACKEND: Host binding registers token and current ownership
    Note over BACKEND,APNS: Provider credentials remain server-side
    BACKEND->>APNS: Send approved alert to matching token/topic/environment
    APNS-->>APP: Best-effort device delivery
    APP->>UI: Presentation callback or user-open response
    UI->>UI: Wait for authorized startup/navigation readiness
    Note over APP,UI: Receipt, display, user open and backend acceptance are separate evidence
```

</MermaidZoom>

1. Build/run the actual non-production scheme on an authorized physical test device. Confirm the signed app identity/environment and provider configuration.
2. Use the approved preference flow and confirm the APNs callback plus **actual backend registration result**, without logging a raw token.
3. Have the authorized backend sender send the synthetic ordinary alert to that installation.
4. Test foreground presentation, background presentation, a warm tap, and a tap that launches a terminated app. Confirm no duplicate local notification.
5. Delay authentication/navigation readiness and confirm the generic open remains pending until accepted.
6. Test denied permission, registration failure, account replacement, and repeated eligible registration. Confirm no stale-account association or false success.

| Observation | Investigate |
| :--- | :--- |
| No registration callback/success | Entitlement/profile, network/device state, and actual activation/lifecycle wiring. |
| Token registered but provider rejects it | APNs topic/bundle ID, environment, token representation, credentials, and classified provider response. |
| Provider accepts but no visible alert | Permission/presentation settings, device state, payload, expiry, and delivery behavior. |
| Foreground alert absent | The template defaults `presentInForeground` to false; inspect the approved preference and delegate callback. |
| Tap does not navigate | Delegate ownership, startup order, pending flag, and the actual acceptance/readiness binding. |
| Previous account remains associated | Host backend lifecycle and in-flight ownership checks; resetting the UI is insufficient. |

APNs is best-effort and may delay, discard, or reorder notifications. Server acceptance does not prove device display or user acknowledgement. [Apple delivery semantics](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/usernotifications/sending-notification-requests-to-apns).

