## 1. Confirm the library version and runtime contract

The documented package is `@mobilearch/enterprise-pushjs`; its examples pass the **AWS SDK for JavaScript v2 `AWS` object** to library methods. This package identifier is a dependency contract, not permission to download it outside the approved registry/account.

AWS SDK v2 reached end of support on **September 8, 2025**. Do not replace `AWS` with an SDK v3 `SNSClient` unless the PushJS owner confirms compatibility for an approved release. New production adoption needs an owner-approved supported-library/migration decision. [AWS v2 support status](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSJavaScriptSDK/latest/file.README.html).

Confirm these items with the package/platform owner before executing the calls:

| Contract | Required answer |
| :--- | :--- |
| Package/runtime compatibility | Exact approved PushJS version, Node version, dependency/peer versions, and AWS SDK support. |
| Method completion | Does each method return a Promise, SDK Request, synchronous value, or fire-and-forget result? How are errors surfaced? |
| Registration output | How is the returned `deviceArn` represented and how are existing/disabled/stale endpoints reconciled? |
| Subscription output | How are single/multiple Subscription ARNs and partial failures returned? |
| Publish outcome | What response proves provider acceptance, and where is the operation/message reference? |
| Retry behavior | Which writes can be retried internally, and how are unknown outcomes reconciled? |
| Payload construction | Supported provider formats, safe `extras`, size limits, and APNs/FCM configuration. |

Only `registerDevice` is shown awaited in the supplied usage contract. The other examples do not establish completion/error return types. Do not add `.promise()`, assume that `await undefined` waits for delivery, or invent response properties to make a wrapper appear complete.

